{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension compared\ncase-insensitively. When validating outline evidence inputs, Habeas MUST report\nthe line of the first syntax error in a recovered outline. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline, except\nthat a Markdown profile MAY additionally recognize colon-fenced directive\ncontainers under {cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.markdown.fence_precedence`\nand expose them as outline nodes under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.markdown.fenced_blocks`. Such containers MUST\npreserve source ranges and containment, and MUST NOT be recognized within\nliteral code or raw HTML. Other nodes MUST be transparent, so their outline\ndescendants appear under the nearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no test-adequacy gap in the supplied tests for `requirements.references.outlines`. I did not request implementation source. The tests cover each obligation as follows.\n\n- **Profiles chosen by extension, case-insensitively:** `profiles_are_selected_by_extension_case_insensitively` in `outline/tests.rs` asserts the profile name for upper- and mixed-case Rust, Python, TSX, Markdown and TOML paths. It also asserts that an extensionless name and a double-extension name get the plain-text profile. CSS, YAML and plain `.ts` are not in that list. They go through the same extension lookup, so I treat that as an untested value in an already represented input class.\n- **Pinned grammars:** `grammar_and_redaction_dependencies_are_pinned_to_exact_versions` asserts that every tree-sitter dependency, including each language grammar, uses an exact version pin in `Cargo.toml`.\n- **Line of the first syntax error when validating outline inputs:**\n  - `reports_recovered_syntax_errors_with_lines` checks Rust, Python, and TOML. The TOML source has a later error as well, and the test asserts that the first one is reported and that the diagnostic text starts with its line.\n  - `outline_inputs_survive_moves_and_unhashed_comments_but_not_edits` in `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs` asserts that validation reports a parse error carrying the line of a broken Rust file.\n- **Plain-text profile:** `plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes` checks that blank lines produce no node and that non-blank lines become anonymous `line@` nodes. It also checks that whitespace is normalised in the selector.\n- **Per-language profiles mapping kinds, with naming rules:**\n  - Per-language tests cover Rust (`rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry`, `selectors_name_syntactic_ancestry_and_duplicates`, `item_macros_are_anonymous_nodes_and_expression_macros_are_not`), Python, TypeScript/TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML.\n  - `markdown_views_respect_profile_field_and_child_naming_rules` covers field and first-child naming, including the case where the field or child is missing.\n- **Only mapped kinds appear; other nodes are transparent:**\n  - `profile_rules_map_only_matching_contexts_and_keep_other_nodes_transparent` shows an unmapped function whose nested mapped function appears under the root. It also checks the missing-selector and malformed-selector errors.\n  - `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds` checks every language for fallback kinds.\n- **Markdown directive containers:**\n  - `paragraphs_and_nested_directives_have_independent_source_ranges` checks source ranges and containment of nested blocks.\n  - `unfinished_directives_retain_their_body_and_leave_the_outer_closer_outside` and `nested_fence_lengths_and_closer_whitespace_preserve_the_right_containers` check unclosed fences, closer lengths and trailing text.\n  - `literal_code_inline_code_and_html_shield_directive_looking_text` checks that directives are not recognised in backtick or tilde code fences, inline code, or raw HTML.\n- **Attached siblings count toward line range and hash:** `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` asserts the line range including the doc comment and attribute. `identity_hashes_include_own_attached_siblings_but_not_descendants` and `python_profile_outlines_defs_classes_and_statements` (decorator range) cover the same behaviour.\n- **Root node and root ownership of unmapped source:**\n  - `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root` covers file-level doc comments and Markdown front matter.\n  - The Python test asserts that a top-level import and a top-level `if` belong to the root.\n  - `rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry` asserts that resolving the empty selector gives the root, and the plain-text test asserts that a blank line belongs to the root.\n- **Comments are not outline nodes:** The CSS, TOML, YAML, Markdown and Rust tests include comments that never appear in the node or selector lists. The Markdown test also asserts that comments are recorded separately.\n- **Profiles documented:** `every_profile_mapping_is_documented` checks that each profile's extensions and each kind rule (kind, grammar node and non-empty naming description) appear in `md/reference.md`. It also checks the plain-text row.\n- **Upgrading is a deliberate change:** The exact-pin test enforces this, because any grammar bump requires editing the pin.\n\nThe assertions are specific enough to reject the plausible violations I considered: a wrong profile choice, fallback node kinds, comments appearing as nodes, directives recognised inside code or HTML, mis-nested directives, and the first syntax error being missed.",
  "references": "9d30947e453ad4064843f4d95ad0930ffb053a14f36fd5ab06ca6bac9e2bda02",
